; The run-time system is C and assembly, built by the Makefile in this
; directory rather than by dune - it has to be cross-compiled for 32-bit x86,
; which dune knows nothing about and which would break "dune build" on a
; machine without the cross toolchain.
;
; What dune does own is *installing* it, so that "dune install" and "dune
; uninstall" cover the run-time as well as the qc binary, rather than leaving
; a pile of files that only a hand-written rule could remove. Everything below
; lands in <prefix>/lib/cmm/, alongside the package's other installed files.
;
; A client - fork-tiger, say - then needs
;
;   qc--runtime.h        to compile its own C against the run-time interface
;   pcmap.h              included by runtime.c
;   pcmap.ld             on its link line; it places the .pcmap sections and
;                        defines Cmm_pc_map and Cmm_pc_map_limit around them
;   the .c/.s/.c-- files and the Makefile, to build the run-time itself
;
; Why the sources and not a prebuilt libqcmm.a: cut.c--, thread.c-- and
; yield.c-- have to be compiled with the *client's* global-variable
; declarations. Every C-- unit that touches the global area references a
; symbol Cmm.globalsig.<hash> computed from those declarations, so an archive
; built for a client with no globals will not link against one that declares
; some - tiger declares "bits32 alloc_ptr;". Until that is resolved there is
; no single archive that is correct for everybody, so we ship what is needed
; to build the right one. See the XXX in this directory's Makefile.
(install
 (section lib)
 (package cmm)
 (files
  qc--runtime.h
  pcmap.h
  pcmap.ld
  runtime.c
  pcmap.c
  gcc-linux.c
  x86cont.s
  cut.c--
  thread.c--
  yield.c--
  Makefile))
